61028275-612a-43a5-b51d-753350b835aa	b37213e0-4b7f-4b07-8fd4-fb6c03b164ce	assistant	I see you've modeled `Produit` with `reference/designation/prixHT` and test instantiation — good start like `Four` description versus real oven.\n\nBut your attributes are `public`, so `p.prixHT = -100.0` bypasses all control, exactly like `four.resistance = null;` destroying the Resistance hidden by the carcasse (Chapter 3, Slide 42). Rule: always `private` by default, expose via Getter/Setter contract with validation (Slides 45-47).\n\nAlso `reference = reference;` assigns the parameter to itself — how could the `this.jour = jour` pattern and a defensive `setPrixHT` check fix construction and negative prices?	{"label": "Chapter 3 (Slide 42): Encapsulation and Visibility", "en_note": "Private carcasse protects internal state; public fields allow external destruction, requiring getter/setter contract.", "pdf_url": "https://peddvhsykuglvicfaezr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/course-pdfs/java_oop/chapters/ch3_les_classes.pdf", "page_number": 42, "chapter_code": "CH3_LES_CLASSES"}	[{"name": "Produit.java", "content": "public class Produit {\\n  public String reference;\\n  public String designation;\\n  public double prixHT;\\n\\n  public Produit(String reference, String designation, double prixHT) {\\n    reference = reference;\\n    designation = designation;\\n    prixHT = prixHT;\\n  }\\n}"}, {"name": "TestProduit.java", "content": "public class TestProduit {\\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\\n    Produit p = new Produit(\\"REF01\\", \\"Clavier\\", 45.0);\\n    p.prixHT = -100.0;\\n    System.out.println(\\"Prix: \\" + p.prixHT);\\n  }\\n}"}]	11732	2026-11-01 10:42:05.165309+00	\N
